## The `mur` CLI

`mur` is Reactor's repo-aware command-line tool. It is the canonical
entry point for the localization workflow and a handful of
repo-maintenance utilities. The subcommands map one-to-one to
the workflows below.

| Subcommand | Purpose | Common invocation |
|------------|---------|-------------------|
| `mur loc` | Run the localization pipeline (`extract`, `translate`, `validate`, `status`, `prune`) | `mur loc extract` |
| `mur devtools` | **Removed** — prints the winapp equivalent of each old command and exits `1` ([mapping](#winui-devtools-winapp)) | `mur devtools --help` |
| `mur check` | Repo-health checks (cref validity, namespace policy, "did you mean" suggestions) | `mur check` |
| `mur doctor` | Verify the install — SDK, `mur`, local feed, templates, plugin | `mur doctor` |
| `mur upgrade` | Re-pack the framework and refresh the plugin after a `git pull`; checks template availability but does not install the pack (that's `winapp new`) | `mur upgrade` |
| `mur figma watch` | Poll a Figma file for design changes | `mur figma watch` |
| `mur pack-local` / `mur clean-local` | Package / clean the local NuGet feed for source-built framework smoke tests; scaffolded apps default to the public Reactor preview unless `--reactor-version` is supplied | `mur pack-local` |
| `mur templates status` | Report whether `dotnet new reactor` resolves (exit `0` available, `1` probe failed, `2` pack too old, `3` pack missing) | `mur templates status` |

To *install* the template pack, use the Windows App SDK CLI rather than `mur`:
`winapp new -t reactor -n MyApp` installs it on demand and scaffolds in one
step, and `winapp new --list` installs it without scaffolding. `bootstrap.ps1`
drives that same command, so a bootstrapped checkout can go straight to
`dotnet new reactor`.

Beyond the subcommands there are four top-level options worth knowing:
`mur --create <Name>` scaffolds a new Reactor project, `mur --skill`
prints the embedded `SKILL.md` agent reference, `mur --api` prints the
`reactor.api.txt` signatures index, and `mur --regen-api` regenerates
that index from a source checkout.

The doc pipeline is not part of `mur`: it is a contributor tool for the
Reactor repository, run from a checkout with
`dotnet run --project tools/Reactor.DocPipeline -- <command>`. Its
commands are `compile` (templates + doc apps → `docs/guide/`, the one you
reach for most often), `check-tier` (tier lint only), `render-diagrams`
and `new-diagram`. See
[the doc-pipeline contributor guide](https://github.com/microsoft/microsoft-ui-reactor/blob/main/docs/contributing/doc-pipeline.md)
for the full surface and the `--validate-only`, `--no-screenshots`
(alias `--skip-screenshots`), `--skip-diagrams`, `--no-build`,
`--skip-reference`, and `--tier <stub|solid|comprehensive>`
flags that make the inner loop fast.

## WinUI DevTools (winapp)

The developer tools for a running Reactor app are the WinUI DevTools in the
[winapp CLI](https://github.com/microsoft/winappCli). Nothing goes in your
project: keep a Debug build (source mapping is on in Debug) and launch with
DevTools attached:

<!-- ai:lock -->
```
winapp run . --devtools --detach
winapp devtools inspect
```
<!-- /ai:lock -->

`inspect` shows the tree your C# created — `<Counter>` component rows, element
kinds, keys, and the `App.cs:67` call site of each element — across every window.
Reactor selectors such as `"<Counter>/Button"`, `key=t1`, and `App.cs:67` work in
both `winapp devtools` and `winapp ui`. AI agents use the same CLI with `--json`
and the `winui-devtools` skill; the MCP server is not carried over. Add `--watch` to `winapp run` to rebuild on rude edits and re-attach DevTools automatically.

| `mur devtools` (removed) | use instead |
|---|---|
| `mur devtools [project]` | `winapp run . --devtools` (add `--watch` for hot reload with automatic re-attach) |
| `session list` / `session clean` | `winapp devtools list` / not needed |
| `version` | `winapp devtools call DevTools.negotiate` (reports the Reactor version) |
| `tree`, `ancestors` | `winapp devtools inspect [<selector> --ancestors]` |
| `properties`, `set-property`, `styles` | `winapp devtools get-property`, `set-property` |
| `resources`, `set-resource` | `winapp devtools get-resource`, `set-resource` |
| `state` | `winapp devtools get-state`, `set-state`, `rerender` (any component) |
| `fire` | `winapp devtools invoke-handler <selector> <event>` (for example `OnClick`) |
| `click`, `type`, `focus`, `invoke`, `scroll`, `wait` | `winapp ui click`, `set-value`, `focus`, `invoke`, `scroll`, `wait-for` |
| `toggle`, `select`, `expand`, `collapse` | `winapp ui invoke <selector> --action toggle` (`select`, `expand`, `collapse`) |
| `screenshot`, `windows`, `windows.list`, `windows.activate` | `winapp ui screenshot`, `winapp ui list-windows`, `winapp ui focus -w <hwnd>` |
| In-app ⚡ dev menu | The DevTools overlay toolbar |
| `--print-config`, `call <tool>` (MCP) | Not carried over: the matching winapp command with `--json` |
| `windows.close`, `shutdown`, `call docking.*` | No tool equivalent: app behavior — drive the app's UI with `winapp ui` |
| `logs` | `winapp devtools logs [--follow]` |
| `reload` | `winapp run . --devtools --watch` |
| References overlay (`call references`) | `winapp devtools get-property <selector>` (References section) |
| `--component`, `components`, `switch`, `windows.open` | `winapp run . --devtools -- --preview <Name>` — the preview host lives in the app, so for now the app must reference `Microsoft.UI.Reactor.Devtools` |

`mur devtools --help` prints the complete mapping. Every `mur devtools` command
prints its winapp equivalent to stderr and exits `1`; none of them runs anything.

## MCP Server

> **Removed.** `mur devtools` no longer launches, supervises or connects to an MCP
> endpoint, `--print-config` is gone, and the in-app MCP server has been removed
> from `Microsoft.UI.Reactor.Devtools` (with the `--mcp-port`, `--mcp-transport`,
> `--devtools-log-level`, `--devtools-logs*` and `--devtools-project` flags and the
> `--devtools app|screenshot|tree` verbs). Use
> [`winapp run . --devtools`](#winui-devtools-winapp) and the winapp CLI with
> `--json`; agents load the `winui-devtools` skill.

## VS Code Panel

The Reactor VS Code extension lives in `src/vscode-reactor/` and provides
a side panel that:

- Lists the running app's component tree.
- Highlights the source line for any element you click in the tree.
- Renders the latest screenshot capture next to your editor (the same
  PNGs the doc pipeline produces).
- Toggles a **References** overlay that draws the app's reactive
  reference graph — `descriptor.Reference`/`.ReferenceList`, the
  `binding.Reference` bridge, and modifier edges such as `.LabeledBy`
  or `.XYFocusDown` — and flags reference cycles and perpetually-null
  (unresolved) references as diagnostics. See
  [focus &amp; input internals](focus-and-input-internals.md).

The extension launches your project with `--devtools run --vscode` and talks
to the in-app host in the deprecated `Microsoft.UI.Reactor.Devtools` package, so
the app must still reference that package for the panel to work. Reactor has
no special editor requirement beyond the standard C# Dev Kit — the
panel is additive. For the interactive Visual Studio alternative that
embeds the real WinUI surface instead of streaming screenshots, see
[Visual Studio Embedded Preview](vs-extension.md).

When you use **Reactor: Connect to Preview** against an already-running
preview process, enter both the `CAPTURE_PORT=...` and `CAPTURE_TOKEN=...`
values from that process's output. The capture server requires the bearer
token before any endpoint probe, including `/status`.

## A Debug-Only Dev Menu

The in-app dev menu (`DevtoolsMenu(...)` and `UseDevtools()`) has been removed.
Inspecting and editing a running app is the job of the WinUI DevTools overlay
toolbar (`winapp run . --devtools`). For app-specific switches — a debug overlay,
a "clear cache" command — build an ordinary menu and compile it into Debug builds
only:

```csharp snippet="dev-tooling/debug-menu"
```

`Observable<T>` is the lightweight INPC cell that backs flags like
`AppFlags.DebugUI`. Declare them as `static readonly` fields:

```csharp snippet="dev-tooling/app-flags"
```

Any component that wants to react to changes subscribes via
`ctx.UseObservable(AppFlags.DebugUI).Value` **during render** — not inside a
menu item callback, which runs when the item is clicked rather than during a
render pass. See [Advanced Patterns](advanced.md) for the broader
observable-binding story.

## Runtime Overlays

- **Reconcile-highlight overlay.** Flashes a rectangle around any
  element that the reconciler patched on the most recent render. Useful
  for catching unintended re-renders (the [Rules of Reactor](rules-of-reactor.md)
  page covers the most common causes). Turn it on from code — for example
  from a Debug-only menu item — with
  `ReactorFeatureFlags.HighlightReconcileChanges = true`. It is zero-cost when off.
- **Keyed-list diagnostics.** `ReactorDiagnostics.RecentKeyedListWarnings`
  holds the most recent keyed-list bailout diagnostics, newest first; surface them from
  the same menu if you want them on screen.
## The Iteration Cycle

The full inner loop:

1. **Run** `dotnet watch run` in your terminal.
2. **Edit** a component in your editor (with the VS Code panel open
   if you want screenshots and the component tree alongside).
3. **Save** the file — `dotnet watch` detects the change and recompiles.
4. **See** the updated UI in the running window.

No build step to invoke manually. The app stays running and the window
stays positioned where you left it.

```csharp snippet="dev-tooling/iteration-demo"
```

![Iteration demo](screenshot://dev-tooling/iteration-demo)

## Patterns

### Per-feature debug flag with a Dev menu toggle

Declare a flag as an `Observable<bool>` in a static class, expose it
through your Debug-only menu's `ToggleMenuItem`, and read it from any component
via `ctx.UseObservable(...)`. Flipping the flag re-renders every
subscribed component — no event-wiring boilerplate. The pattern scales
to a dozen flags without a config UI.

### Headless screenshot capture for docs

The same devtools plumbing the doc pipeline uses is available to
your own app: enable `Reactor.DevtoolsSupport` in the app project and
launch the app with `--devtools run` so the screenshot harness can
capture the window. See
[`docs/contributing/doc-pipeline.md`](https://github.com/microsoft/microsoft-ui-reactor/blob/main/docs/contributing/doc-pipeline.md)
for the harness contract. This is how every page in the docset gets
its screenshots automatically without an author launching the app by
hand.

## Common Mistakes

### Shipping the devtools switch in Release

Reference the deprecated `Microsoft.UI.Reactor.Devtools` package in Debug only, so
Release/AOT binaries keep the preview host trimmed; a reference outside Debug gets
build warning `REACTOR_DEVTOOLS_001`. The `Reactor.DevtoolsSupport` switch follows
`ReactorDiagnostics`: on in Debug, and in Release only with
`-p:ReactorDiagnostics=true`, which embeds diagnostics you should not distribute.
The WinUI DevTools need no package.

### Wiring real app state through `UseState` during inner-loop work

`UseState` resets on every hot reload. If your loop depends on a
30-second login flow to reach the screen you are iterating on, store
the relevant state in [`UsePersisted`](persistence.md) (Window scope)
or in an `Observable<T>` field. The hot reload picks up the new code
without resetting your state.

### Expecting the WinUI DevTools under a bare `dotnet watch run`

The WinUI DevTools attach only when you launch through
`winapp run . --devtools` (add `--watch` for hot reload with automatic
re-attach). A bare `dotnet watch run` starts the app without them.
## Tips

**Keep dotnet watch running.** Don't stop and restart it between
edits. It handles recompilation and reconnection automatically.

**Use small components.** Smaller components reload faster because
less of the tree needs to be rebuilt. Extract pieces early.

**Check the terminal.** When hot reload fails (usually a syntax
error), `dotnet watch` prints the error. Fix it and save — it retries.

**Use [function components](components.md) for experiments.**
`ReactorApp.Run("Test", ctx => ...)` is the fastest way to try an
idea. No class boilerplate.

**ARM64 builds for benchmarks.** If you're on ARM64 hardware, build
with `dotnet run -r win-arm64` to get native performance numbers.

